{
  "image_name": "2a0100b735a86b3e87f6090a1b7ff147.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how the tumour microenvironment can drive cancer-associated cachexia through inflammatory signaling. At the top, the tumour microenvironment is shown as a central source of diverse stromal and immune cell populations. These include tumour-associated macrophages, tumour-infiltrating immune cells such as lymphocytes, T cells, B cells, NK cells, granulocytes, mast cells and monocytes, as well as tumour-associated neutrophils, myeloid-derived suppressor cells, cancer-associated adipocytes, and cancer-associated fibroblasts.\n\nAcross these cell types, the main visible message is that many release overlapping inflammatory mediators. Repeatedly shown factors include IL-6, TNF, IL-1, IL-8, IFNγ, VEGF, CCL2, CXCL1, CXCL4, and FAS ligand. Some cells are also divided into contrasting phenotypes, such as M1 versus M2 macrophages and N1 versus N2 neutrophils, suggesting functional heterogeneity within the tumour-associated immune response.\n\nAt the bottom, the diagram condenses this into a causal flow: cancer cells and the wider tumour macroenvironment feed into inflammatory mediators, and these mediators are linked to cancer-associated cachexia. Overall, the figure emphasizes that cachexia is not driven by cancer cells alone, but by a network of tumour-associated immune and stromal cells producing inflammatory signals.",
